%[text] %[text:anchor:T_8A8B1944] # Two-dimensional bilinear basis functions
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] Construction of the bilinear two-dimensional basis functions using a tensor product of the one-dimensional linear [Lagrange polynomials](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lagrange_polynomial).
%[text] 
%[text] Go to [THIS](file:.\main_Chapter1_BasisFunctions.m) Section in the main driver script *One-dimensional quadratic basis functions*
%[text] Go to [PREVIOUS](file:.\main_Chapter12_OneDimensionalQuadraticBasisFunctions.m) Section One\*-dimensional bilinear basis functions\*
%[text] Go to [NEXT](file:.\main_Chapter14_TwoDimensionalBiquadraticBasisFunctions.m) Section *Two-dimensional bilinear basis functions*
%[text] 
%[text] **Author: Dr.-Ing. Andreas Apostolatos**
%[text] **Date: 22.12.2022**
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F203EBCC] ## Preamble
vars = "mshQ1";
str = "This mlx-file should be run from parent mlx-file " + ...
    "main_Chapter01_OneDimensionalLinearBasisFunctions.mlx";
for ii = 1:length(vars)
    if ~exist(vars(ii), 'var') 
        error(strcat("Variable ", vars(ii), " is not defined. ", str));
    end
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_11551A14] ## Definition of the bilinear Lagrange Polynomials
%[text] These Lagrange polynomials are constructed as a tensor product of the one-dimensional linear Lagrange polynomials, namely,
%[text] $l\_{i,j}^{\\text{Q}\_1}(x,y) = l^{L\_1}\_i(\\xi) l^{L\_1}\_j(\\eta)$
%[text] where the superscripts $\\text{L}\_1$ and $\\text{Q}\_1$ refer to one-dimensional linear and two-dimensional bilinear Lagrange polynomials.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![bilinear_mesh.png](text:image:11f0)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_38A24645] ## Set-up Set-up the two-dimensional bilinear Lagrange Polynomials in physical space
%[text] $l^{\\text{Q}\_1}\_{i,j}(X, Y) = \\prod\_{1 \\le m \\le 2, m \\neq j} \\frac{X - X\_m}{X\_i - X\_m} \\prod\_{1 \\le n \\le 2, n \\neq j} \\frac{Y - Y\_n}{Y\_i - Y\_n}$
%[text] ![](text:image:8bb6) **Reflect**
%[text] Can this product rule in the physical space be used to construct an unstructured quadrilateral mesh?
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_748872F1] ### Choice of element for displaying the basis functions in the physical space
elQ1 = 3; %[text:anchor:H_3BC278FD]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_32BFACB1] ### Visualization of the bilinear basis functions in the physical space of the selected element in the bilinear quadratic mesh
t2 = plot2dBilinearLagrangePolynomialsOnPhysicalSpace ... %[text:anchor:H_86824143] %[output:group:837d175d] %[output:3d0f5fea]
    (elQ1, mshQ1, numVertices, l1L1, l2L1, ij); %[output:group:837d175d] %[output:3d0f5fea]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8398C582] ## Two-dimensional bilinear Lagrange Polynomials in parametric space
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_FC8D6877] The parametric space in this case is given as $(\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[-1, 1\]^2$, leveraging symmetry, see the figure below:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![map_to_parametric_space_bilinear.png](text:image:8f07)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0DF769D4] ### Set-up of the two-dimensional bilinear Lagrange Polynomials in the parametric space
syms l11Q1(xi, eta) l12Q1(xi, eta) l22Q1(xi, eta) l21Q1(xi, eta)
l11Q1(xi, eta) = l1L1(xi, -1, +1)*l1L1(eta, -1, +1);
l12Q1(xi, eta) = l1L1(xi, -1, +1)*l2L1(eta, -1, +1);
l22Q1(xi, eta) = l2L1(xi, -1, +1)*l2L1(eta, -1, +1);
l21Q1(xi, eta) = l2L1(xi, -1, +1)*l1L1(eta, -1, +1);
lijQ1 = {l11Q1, l12Q1, l22Q1, l21Q1};
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9A4DBA2D] ### Display the two-dimensional bilinear Lagrange Polynomials in the parametric space
%[text] $N\_1^{Q\_1}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4} \\left( 1 - \\xi \\right) \\left( 1 - \\eta \\right), \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[-1, 1\]^2, \\\\\nN\_2^{Q\_1}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4} \\left( 1 - \\xi \\right) \\left( 1 + \\eta \\right), \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[-1, 1\]^2, \\\\\nN\_3^{Q\_1}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4} \\left( 1 + \\xi \\right) \\left( 1 + \\eta \\right), \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[-1, 1\]^2, \\\\\nN\_4^{Q\_1}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4} \\left( 1 + \\xi \\right) \\left( 1 - \\eta \\right), \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[-1, 1\]^2,$
t3 = tiledlayout(2, 2); %[output:5afb47c5]
for ii = 1:numel(lijQ1)
    nexttile %[output:5afb47c5]
    fsurf(lijQ1{ii}, [-1 1 -1 1], "FaceColor", [217, 218, 219]./255, ...
        "FaceAlpha", 0.5);
    xlabel("\xi")
    ylabel("\eta")
    zlabel(sprintf("l_{%s}", num2str(ij(ii))))
end
title(t3, "Bilinear Lagrange Polynomials"); %[output:5afb47c5]

